#   scripts/gizmo-perf/mutate.py              bb9206fea91190412226ea80b9971a8abd42fa44 (be0fbb44a904957f5276eb406f14f8fd600d653d)
# audit.py, budgets.py and corpus.py are those of ca66012, byte for byte.
# MACHINE LOAD (load averages 1/5/15 min): old spec + old runners 17.79 20.54 17.57 when it began, 25.72 22.24 18.34 after; new spec + OLD runners 25.72 22.24 18.34 when it began, 85.00 41.43 26.09 after; new spec + new runners 85.00 41.43 26.09 when it began, 64.41 48.08 30.48 after. Other work was running on the machine.
# NOTHING WAS MEASURED: no time run, no A/B, no time mutant, no native window, no build, no performance lock, no benchmark.
# This is a test of the runners' bookkeeping on fakes, on throwaway git repositories and on real processes that refuse.
# The control, on the same layout:
#   old spec + OLD runners : Ran 221 tests in 45.27s -> OK  (the layout works)
#   new spec + OLD runners : Ran 252 tests in 103.437s -> FAILED (failures=56, errors=13)
#   new spec + new runners : Ran 252 tests in 95.834s -> OK
# 31 tests are new in this round (ids that the spec of ca66012 does not have); 0 earlier id(s) were removed; the other 221 are the earlier ones.
# Per test: 26 of the 31 new tests fail on the old runners, 5 pass on both sides on purpose (listed after the list below), and 10 EARLIER tests fail on the old runners: those are the ones rewritten on purpose for the
# shared lock name (they named `.mutate.lock`, `.ab.lock`, `.run.lock` or `.native.lock` or the old `guarded` signature; listed separately).
# The counts of unittest are per failing assertion (a failing subTest counts once each).
# Where the behaviour can be seen through a runner's `main` or a real process the new test fails on its own assertion on the old runners;
# the tests of helpers that did not exist (`git_env`, `git_run`, `DIRTY_PATHSPECS`, `OUT_LOCK`, `page_hidden_by_dialog`) can only fail
# there by not finding them. What proves that these tests also tell a right behaviour from a wrong one is the third part: deliberately
# WRONG versions of the fixes, each caught by at least one test.
# Labels: R1 = git runs with the caller's GIT_DIR/GIT_WORK_TREE/GIT_INDEX_FILE; R2 = `dirty` misses .cargo, rust-toolchain.toml, vendor, runtime;
# R3 = one lock file per runner; R4 = `mutate.py time` never looks at the current tree; R5 = `overlay_ack` default 90 s timeout and a late
# matching acknowledgement accepted; sweep (c) = the same two defects in the dialog wait and the job wait of `run_corpus`;
# sweep (d) = `settle_commit` accepts a MATCHING acknowledgement first seen after `ACK_SECONDS`.

